VERDICT: FAIL — unlisted coverage gaps remain; the 864-row total and gate pins are correct.

**TASK 1.** All pass/fail conclusions below are from source tracing, not mutation runs.

Five X14 assertion locations changed, affecting eight emitted rows; one added assertion runs twice. “DEVINIT variants” below means both “DEVINIT timers” and “DEVINIT timers, a tick pending.”

Each replacement is independently applied to the indicated device line.

| Changed/added X14 row | One-line device change that makes it fail |
|---|---|
| DEVINIT variants: five legal reads, test line 1414 | Replace device line 572 with `{ odata = d->irq_status & d->irq_enable; if (d->timer_control[0]) INTERRUPT_ASSERT(d->irq); }`. Violates the new `ZCPU(0,0)`. |
| DEVINIT variants: Set then Clear, line 1420 | Replace line 616 with `{ if (!d->timer_control[0]) INTERRUPT_DEASSERT(d->irq); }`. Observes `(1,0)` instead of `(1,1)`. |
| DEVINIT variants: five declines, lines 1425–1426 | Replace line 577 with `d->warned_irq_status_w = 1; if (d->timer_control[0]) INTERRUPT_ASSERT(d->irq);`. Violates `ZCPU(0,0)`. |
| Device-raised line 4: IRQ_STATUS read, lines 1446–1448 | Replace line 572 with `{ odata = d->irq_status & d->irq_enable; d->irq_asserted = 0; }`. Fails the new post-read `irq_asserted == 1` check. |
| Device-raised line 4: re-arm sequence, lines 1476–1477 | Replace line 584 with `if (writeflag == MEM_READ && !(d->irq_asserted && d->warned_irq_raw_w))`. The latched IRQ_RAW_STATUS read returns zero instead of the newly checked `0x10`. |
| **Added:** DEVINIT variants: Clear `0x20`, line 1435 | Replace line 614 with `{ if (!d->timer_control[0]) INTERRUPT_ASSERT(d->irq); }`. Suppresses the required CPU assert. |

**There is an overclaim:** the added rows say “keeps `0x10` pending and enabled,” but assert only the enable value and callback counts—not preservation of `irq_status`. The re-arm row also omits final FIQ-field checks. See [X14 assertions](C:/DocumentNoSnc/cc/GXEMUL/_scratchpad/fbd5/q2f/frozen/diff_footbridge.c:1435).

**TASK 2.** The smallest defined-behavior escape I found adds six non-whitespace characters to the IRQ_STATUS latch guard at device line 573:

```c
else if (!(d->warned_irq_status_w++&3)) {
```

Keep the existing `warned_irq_status_w = 1` body unchanged.

- Declines 1–4 report `1,0,0,0`, matching the original.
- Decline 5 reports again; the original remains silent.
- No instance in this test reaches five declines of that site. P1 reaches four; the other blocks reach fewer.
- No assertion inspects the latch’s numeric value. Consequently, all 864 rows retain their original results.
- The counter resets before overflow. This is a missing repeated-access history, not an untested register-value predicate or different Boolean latch combination named in NOT COVERED.

There is also a direct guest-visible escape demonstrating Task 1’s overclaim. Replace the Clear case’s assert statement at device line 614 with:

```c
{ INTERRUPT_ASSERT(d->irq); if (d->timer_control[0]) d->irq_status = 0; }
```

Only the two added DEVINIT Clear-assert rows exercise that state-changing branch. Both still pass: enable remains `0x10`, one CPU assert occurs, and neither checks status afterward. A subsequent guest IRQ_STATUS read returns zero instead of `0x10`. This gap occurs **inside the sampled X14 cells**, so the “beyond X14’s cells” exclusion does not cover it.

